can you summarize  for our viewers the impact of omicron in  your country thus far how many infected  how many hospitalizations um and how  many deaths  yeah good morning uh trevor  yeah i think it's been a kind of a mixed  bag in a way because basically on the  one hand we've had a very rapid rising  uh rate of infections in fact if you  look at the epidemic epidemic curve it's  almost vertical  so steep as it's going up in fact our  doubling time at the moment  is uh about one to one point five days  it's in other words every one and a half  days we're doubling in numbers so it's  it's rapidly increasing that's on the  one hand  on the other hand our hospitalization  rates have not been that high uh and  also within hospitalized uh within  hospitalized individuals uh it's only  about 15 percent i think is the latest  deity we've got  of those hospitalized are actually  either in icu or in high care so it  looks so far that the  the clinical expression has been mild  but that could really be  due to the fact that immunity in the  country is high either from natural  immunity or past infection uh or from  the uh from vaccinations so that's the  other so that's the kind of positive  side the rate of transmission is the  negative side um and the one other  negative that does appear to be quite  quite apparent  is that there's a lot of what we call  vaccine escape a lot of people that have  been fully vaccinated are still getting  infected but fortunately of that group  the great majority are mild so that's a  kind of a round  overall picture where we are at the  moment  that's very helpful um  the population of south africa is  on average much younger than the  population here and our  cabinet minister secretary of state for  education who i spoke to earlier in the  program  painted a picture of exponential growth  of omicron here which  at one level is pretty terrifying and he  said that even if it is milder as you  seem to be suggesting from the data in  south africa we could still end up with  a really large number of cases bearing  in mind  that uh we have you know many more older  people um is that something that we  ought to be alarmed about  you know actually i don't want to give  the the kind of wrong impression that  the omicron is causing milder disease i  think what we're seeing there's a lot  more about it but that's i think because  of immunity  uh it's very early days at this stage  and i don't think at this stage we can  be confident in saying omicron is a  milder variant we hope it's going to be  but i don't think we can say and you  know if you look at the virus itself  very logically to date there's no real  evidence to suggest that will be milder  so you make a very good point if there's  a higher proportion of older individuals  it may well not express itself quite as  much as we've seen quite in the same way  as you see in south africa but i must  what one thing i should point out though  is that we are seeing more and more  cases in younger individuals in fact  even young children as  young as two years of old age we've seen  quite some serious cases  so we need to kind of take that into the  balance as well  so would your advice to us be  don't get ahead of yourself  treat this as  being as serious potentially as delta or  any other variant we've encountered thus  far  i think we need to i think we need to  i think we must avoid trivializing it at  this stage we're still gathering  information we're hoping it's going to  be a time advice but we really cannot  say so at this stage so at the moment we  do need to treat it as seriously as  delta with all the kind of precautions  that we took for delta we can't let up  on that at the moment  have you got any thoughts or any idea  about how long before we'll get some  hard enough data to be able to make  you know proper  sense of how serious this thing really  is  yeah we were all we are gathering data  in fact day by day week by week  so i would guess that certainly in the  next couple of weeks which we should  have a much better uh perspective on the  virus uh we must remember we've only had  this virus for about three or four weeks  so it's still really early days  restaurant in south africa on the upward  trajectory of our fourth wave so we  still need to kind of be cautious  hopefully three or four weeks time we'll  have a much better view of it  so we may know something by the new year  um  our government is saying and i heard it  again this morning from from our  minister  that uh  there is evidence that  the  booster jab uh has  a significant impact on the virulence of  omicron is that what you're seeing in  south africa  yes i think the booster certainly will  will have a significant effect you know  it's only relative that there is vaccine  escape so obviously the more we  stimulate the immune system we can kind  of get a higher level of immunity to  overcome this potential vaccine escape  in south africa we're not we haven't yet  we're not nearly as extensive  in boosting  as you are for example the united  kingdom so we wouldn't really be able to  kind of give data at this stage on that  professor thank you so much for your  information valuable and  fascinating as ever  thank you trevor thank you

